<section id="harness" class="section" aria-labelledby="harness-title">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <h2 id="harness-title">07 Harness with Policy</h2>
    <section
      id="harness-setting"
      class="case-study-section"
      aria-labelledby="harness-setting-title"
    >
      <h3 id="harness-setting-title">Evaluation Setting</h3>
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          The comparisons above suggest complementary strengths: GPT-6-Astra interprets tasks and
          recovers disrupted goals, whereas post-trained policies execute precise and bimanual
          contact more reliably. The harness with policy combines the two. Following{' '}
          <a
            class="source-link"
            href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08448"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
          >
            Harness VLA
          </a>
          , implemented in RPent{' '}
          <a class="paper-citation" href="#ref-rpent" role="doc-biblioref" aria-label="Reference 1">
            [1]
          </a>
          , it exposes a frozen post-trained policy as a callable tool alongside the EEF waypoint
          interface, together with a call that returns the annotated demonstration on request. In
          each invocation, GPT-6-Astra assigns the policy 1–300 physics steps. Within these steps,
          the policy acts without intervention from the agent and then returns new visual and
          proprioceptive observations.
        </p>
        <p>
          We evaluate two policies post-trained on EBench demonstrations across all 510 test
          episodes: the VLA π₀.₅, which acts on the current observation and replans every 30 physics
          steps, and the WAM InternW0-Δ, which also conditions on two earlier frames and replans
          every 10 physics steps. Evaluated alone, π₀.₅ trails GPT-6-Astra on both metrics, and
          InternW0-Δ exceeds it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section
      id="harness-results"
      class="case-study-section"
      aria-labelledby="harness-results-title"
    >
      <h3 id="harness-results-title">Overall Performance</h3>
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          Each harness configuration outperforms both of its components. Overall SR rises from 46.7%
          under direct control to <strong>53.0% with π₀.₅ and 56.0% with InternW0-Δ</strong>, and
          the Score from 0.654 to 0.692 and 0.744. With InternW0-Δ, the harness surpasses OpenWAM-α,
          the strongest post-trained policy, on both metrics. The gains concentrate on long-horizon
          and tabletop tasks, where the policies alone outperform direct control, whereas on
          high-precision tasks both configurations remain below the policies alone. Invoking a
          policy also reduces the token cost per success from 8.1M under direct control to 3.5M with
          π₀.₅ and 4.0M with InternW0-Δ.
        </p>
      </div>
